{"approved_content_sha256":"0ee9eefb579d3cb4d3294b65b5953383c05e5d201356cbe5e2b6e5fc2e5edd59","candidate_id":"candidate.stacks.08a95c1aa3410b174d7fc97d","decision":"source_equivalence_approved","final_content":{"candidate_id":"candidate.stacks.08a95c1aa3410b174d7fc97d","conclusions":["$A \to B$ is ind-Zariski."],"equivalence_sides":null,"hypotheses":["Let $A \to B$ be ring map such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item $A \to B$ identifies local rings,
\item the topological spaces $\Spec(B)$, $\Spec(A)$ are w-local, and
\item $\Spec(B) \to \Spec(A)$ is w-local.
\end{enumerate}"],"logical_form":"single_implication","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","statement":"**Source terminology (not extra hypotheses).** A **w-local** spectral space has closed set of closed points and every point specializes to a unique closed point; a w-local map between such spaces is spectral and sends closed points to closed points. A ring map $A\to B$ is a **local isomorphism** when near every prime of $B$ it induces an open immersion after inverting an element of $B$; it **identifies local rings** when $A_{q\cap A}\to B_q$ is an isomorphism for every prime $q$ of $B$. A ring map is **ind-Zariski** when its target is a filtered colimit of local-isomorphism algebras over the source.

Let $A \to B$ be ring map such that
