formulation.statement:
For a unital map of commutative rings $u:R\to S$, going down means: for every chain $\mathfrak p\subset\mathfrak p^\prime$ in $\operatorname{Spec}R$ and $\mathfrak q^\prime$ over $\mathfrak p^\prime$, there exists $\mathfrak q\subset\mathfrak q^\prime$ over $\mathfrak p$. Going up means: for every such chain and $\mathfrak q$ over $\mathfrak p$, there exists $\mathfrak q^\prime\supset\mathfrak q$ over $\mathfrak p^\prime$. Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Going up holds for $R \to S$, and
\item the map $\Spec(S) \to \Spec(R)$ is closed.
\end{enumerate}

source.original_statement_tex:
\label{lemma-going-up-closed}
Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Going up holds for $R \to S$, and
\item the map $\Spec(S) \to \Spec(R)$ is closed.
\end{enumerate}

