\label{lemma-descend-syntomic}
Notation and assumptions as in Situation \ref{situation-descent-property}.
If
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ is syntomic, and
\item $f_0$ is locally of finite presentation,
\end{enumerate}
then there exists an $i \geq 0$ such that $f_i$ is syntomic.

\label{lemma-fibre-product-finite-type}
Let $R$ be a ring. Let $A \to B$ and $C \to B$ be $R$-algebra maps.
Assume
\begin{enumerate}
\item $R$ is Noetherian,
\item $A$, $B$, $C$ are of finite type over $R$,
\item $A \to B$ is surjective, and
\item $B$ is finite over $C$.
\end{enumerate}
Then $A \times_B C$ is of finite type over $R$.

