Here $\mathbf P(\mathcal E)=\underline{\operatorname{Proj}}_X(\operatorname{Sym}^*\mathcal E)$ and invertible quotients are taken up to isomorphism commuting with the quotient map. For a quasi-coherent nonnegatively graded algebra $\mathcal A$ on S, $\underline{\operatorname{Proj}}_S(\mathcal A)$ is formed by gluing affine Proj constructions; its twists $\mathcal O_X(d)$ are locally the sheaves associated to the shifted graded module $\mathcal A(d)$, $\mathcal A(d)_n=\mathcal A_{n+d}$, and $\mathcal G(d)=\mathcal G\otimes\mathcal O_X(d)$. Indices of twists are integers. For an immersion $i:Z\to X$, choose an open $V\subset X$ where it is closed, with ideal $\mathcal I$; the conormal algebra $\mathcal C_{Z/X,*}$ has degree $n$ piece corresponding to $\mathcal I^n/\mathcal I^{n+1}$ on $Z$, and $\mathcal C_{Z/X}=\mathcal C_{Z/X,1}$. Its canonical map from $\operatorname{Sym}^*(\mathcal C_{Z/X})$ is induced by multiplication of ideals. Let $X$ be a scheme. Let $\mathcal{E}$ be a quasi-coherent
$\mathcal{O}_X$-module. There is a bijection
$$
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\text{sections }\sigma\text{ of the } \\
\text{morphism } \mathbf{P}(\mathcal{E}) \to X
\end{matrix}
\right\}
\leftrightarrow
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\text{surjections }\mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{L}\text{ where} \\
\mathcal{L}\text{ is an invertible }\mathcal{O}_X\text{-module}
\end{matrix}
\right\}
$$
In this case $\sigma$ is a closed immersion and there is a canonical
isomorphism
$$
\Ker(\mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{L})
\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_X} \mathcal{L}^{\otimes -1}
\longrightarrow
\mathcal{C}_{\sigma(X)/\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{E})}
$$
Both the bijection and isomorphism are compatible with base change.

